    "text": "Chemistry of Acid Halides"
  },
  "documentClass": null,
  "metadata": {
    "uuid": "d574f92f-a11d-4b3f-abf7-432d7bc18cba"
  },
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00001",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00242:title:sect-00001-title",
        "text": "Preparation of Acid Halides"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00001",
          "segmentId": "m00242:para:para-00001"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00001",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00242:alt:fig-00001-alt",
              "text": "The first reaction shows conversion of a carboxylic acid to an acid chloride using thionyl chloride. The second reaction shows conversion of a carboxylic acid to an acid bromide using phosphorus tribromide in ether."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_04_001.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00002",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00242:title:sect-00002-title",
        "text": "Reactions of Acid Halides"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00002",
          "segmentId": "m00242:para:para-00002"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00002",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00242:alt:fig-00002-alt",
              "text": "The reaction schemes for converting an acid chloride to an acid using water, acid anhydride using carboxylate ion, ester using alcohol, amide using ammonia, and ketone using diorganocopper-lithium reagents."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_04_002.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "id": "sect-00003",
          "class": null,
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00242:title:sect-00003-title",
            "text": "Conversion of Acid Halides into Carboxylic Acids: Hydrolysis"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00003",
              "segmentId": "m00242:para:para-00003"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00003",
              "class": "unnumbered",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00242:alt:fig-00003-alt",
                  "text": "Mechanism for the reaction of acid chloride with water to give carboxylic acid and H Cl via a tetrahedral intermediate. Water attacks, chloride leaves, deprotonation by base generates neutral compound."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_21_04_003.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00004",
              "segmentId": "m00242:para:para-00004"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "id": "sect-00004",
          "class": null,
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00242:title:sect-00004-title",
            "text": "Conversion of Acid Halides into Anhydrides"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00005",
              "segmentId": "m00242:para:para-00005"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00004",
              "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00242:alt:fig-00004-alt",
                  "text": "The reaction of sodium formate with acetyl chloride in ether at twenty-five degrees Celsius forms acetic formic anhydride (sixty-four percent). The product structure has two carbonyl groups attached to a central oxygen atom."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_21_04_004.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "id": "sect-00005",
          "class": null,
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00242:title:sect-00005-title",
            "text": "Conversion of Acid Halides into Esters: Alcoholysis"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00006",
              "segmentId": "m00242:para:para-00006"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00005",
              "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00242:alt:fig-00005-alt",
                  "text": "The reaction of benzoyl chloride with cyclohexanol in pyridine forms cyclohexyl benzoate (ninety-seven percent). The product has a cyclohexane ring attached to the oxygen atom of a benzoate group."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_21_04_005.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00007",
              "segmentId": "m00242:para:para-00007"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00006",
              "class": "unnumbered",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00242:alt:fig-00006-alt",
                  "text": "The reaction of 4-(hydroxymethyl)cyclohexanol with acetyl chloride in pyridine, giving the product  where a methyl acetate group is attached to a 4-hydroxycyclohexane ring. The primary alcohol group of the starting material reacts and not the secondary alcohol group."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_21_04_006.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "id": "sect-00006",
          "class": "section-exercises",
          "title": null,
          "content": [
