            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "id": "sect-00007",
          "class": null,
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00242:title:sect-00007-title",
            "text": "Conversion of Acid Halides into Amides: Aminolysis"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00010",
              "segmentId": "m00242:para:para-00010"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00007",
              "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00242:alt:fig-00007-alt",
                  "text": "In the first reaction, 2-methylpropanoyl chloride reacts with two moles of ammonia, giving 2-methylpropanamide and ammonium chloride. In the second reaction, benzoyl chloride reacts with two moles of dimethylamine giving N, N-dimethylbenzamide, and dimethylammonium chloride."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_21_04_007.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00011",
              "segmentId": "m00242:para:para-00011"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00008",
              "class": "unnumbered",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00242:alt:fig-00008-alt",
                  "text": "The reaction between 3,4,5-trimethoxybenzoyl chloride and morpholine in the presence of aqueous sodium hydroxide gives trimetozine and sodium chloride. Trimetozine is an amide containing C O N bond."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_21_04_008.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "example",
              "id": "exam-00001",
              "title": {
                "segmentId": "m00242:example-title:exam-00001-title",
                "text": "Synthesizing an Amide from an Acid Chloride"
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "para",
                  "id": "para-00012",
                  "segmentId": "m00242:para:para-00012"
                },
                {
                  "type": "para",
                  "id": "para-00013",
                  "segmentId": "m00242:para:para-00013",
                  "title": {
                    "segmentId": "m00242:para-title:para-00013-title",
                    "text": "Strategy"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "para",
                  "id": "para-00014",
                  "title": {
                    "segmentId": "m00242:para-title:para-00014-title",
                    "text": "Solution"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00009",
              "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00242:alt:fig-00009-alt",
                  "text": "Propanoyl chloride reacts with two moles of methylamine, forming N-methylpropanamide and methylammonium chloride."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_21_04_009.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
